INT. HOSPITAL CONSULTATION ROOM, ELEVENTH FLOOR - MORNING

Pale light through venetian blinds. A box of tissues, deliberately placed. DR. ANAND PILLAI (50s, soft-spoken, cardigan under his coat) turns a folder over in his hands. NURSE BRIDGET MOONEY (40s, quiet) stands by the door. CASIMIR WOJTYLA (70s, retired tailor, pressed shirt) sits very straight, hands folded over his cap.

CASIMIR
My scans look good, yes? You called me in early only to tell me to stop worrying.

PILLAI
Mr. Wojtyla, thank you for coming in.

BRIDGET
Can I get you some water?

CASIMIR
Water, no. I would like the news. The weather I can see for myself.

PILLAI
The scans were clear in the lungs.

CASIMIR
There. You see, Bridget? I told you the cough was the cold weather.

BRIDGET
You did.

PILLAI
Mr. Wojtyla, the scan did find something else.

CASIMIR
What something?

PILLAI
A shadow on the liver. Two, in fact.

CASIMIR
Shadows. I have spent forty years looking at shadows on cloth. They come out with a good steam.

BRIDGET
Mr. Wojtyla--

PILLAI
^These will not come out with steam, I am afraid.

CASIMIR
So. It is that kind of shadow.

PILLAI
Yes.

CASIMIR
And how long does that kind of shadow give a man?

PILLAI
Months. Perhaps more, with treatment.

CASIMIR
Months. (beat) Thank you for saying it plainly. Bridget, you may stop holding the door. I am not going to run.
